
REVISED PROJECT SPECIFICATION & FEATURE OUTLINE


1. CORE ARCHITECTURAL RULES

• Path Management: The use of automatic variables like $PSScriptRoot is strictly prohibited across all script modules to ensure path safety across different running contexts.
• Threading Rule: Long-running operations, network commands, and folder scans must run inside the background runspace pool ($Global:NetPoolTracker) to prevent interface freezing.
• Data Isolation: UI paint events read exclusively from memory data caches. Direct system queries inside paint handlers are prohibited to eliminate micro-stutters.
• Advanced Functionality: [CmdletBinding()] must be implemented at the top of all major logic functions and operational tools to support unified parameter validation, standardized error handling, and robust verbose logging switches (-Verbose, -Debug).

2. SEE TOPOLOGY

3. PROGRESSIVE DISCLOSURE MATRIX (COMPACT VS. EXPANDED MODES)

Quadrant 1: Compute Cluster

• Compact Mode: Total CPU load bar, overall GPU load bar, package temperature, OS caption text.
• Expanded Mode: Per-core utilization list, core clock frequency metrics (MHz), GPU Hotspot sensor values, VRAM memory controller utilization percentage, GPU Hardware Accelerated Scheduling (HAGS) registry status indicator and active on/off toggle.

Quadrant 2: Memory & Kernel

• Compact Mode: Physical RAM usage track (Used/Total GB), active power scheme name string, virtualization state (AMD-V / Intel-VT), system uptime counter tracking string.
• Expanded Mode: Active commit charge percentage, non-paged pool allocation size, paged pool allocation size, total OS thread count, active handle count allocation.

Quadrant 3: Storage & Volumetric

• Compact Mode: Logical drive volume listings (C:, D:, etc.), drive type category tag (Fixed/Removable), free capacity vs. total partition size.
• Expanded Mode: Controller interface type (NVMe speed standard vs. SATA), S.M.A.R.T. life wear remaining percentage, active read/write queue depths, Total Bytes Written (TBW) wear counters.

Quadrant 4: Power & Infrastructure

• Compact Mode: Motherboard model name, chassis status tracking codes.
• Expanded Mode: Motherboard VRM sensor temperatures, input power voltage rails (+12V, +5V, +3.3V tracking rows), EPS CPU power draw wattage tracking.

Wide Sub-Console Panel: Network Matrix

• Compact Mode: Internal IP, subnet mask, default gateway, public external IP, network profile status (Private/Public), firewall network discovery state toggle, wireless WLAN SSID connection string.
• Expanded Mode: Active link connection speed (e.g., 10 Gbps), active throughput bitrates (RX/TX), dropped packet counters, transaction retry rates.

4. MODULAR TOOL ARCHITECTURE SPECIFICATIONS


File Organizer Engine

• Monitored Directories: ..\User\%username%\Downloads and ..\User\%username%\Documents by default, with full runtime support to link any targeted directory path.
• Sorting Matrix: Parses targets and groups them into automated subfolders by file extensions:
	• Text: .txt, .pdf, .doc, .docx
	• Code: .ps1, .psm1, .py, .json, .xml, .bat
	• Images: .png, .jpg, .jpeg, .gif, .ico
	• Video: .mp4, .mkv, .avi, .mov
	• Audio: .mp3, .wav, .flac
	• Executables: .exe, .msi

IP / Port Scanner

• Sweeps defined local subnets using multi-threaded asynchronous network connectivity probes.
• Tests validation states across critical infrastructure and data ports (e.g., 135, 445, 3389).

Super Logger

• Targets, captures, and consolidates diverse Windows event channels and application error log streams into a single unified chronological trace table.
• Translates hex-based Win32 error codes directly into human-readable text descriptors.
• Provides a raw log text viewer panel built directly into the module.

File Renamer

• Handles bulk string formatting operations across directory targets.
• Configures naming manipulation via testing prefix strings, string suffix attachments, and sequential renaming masks.

Jitterbug

• Tracks execution discrepancies, latency stutters, and hardware interrupt spikes.
• Measures micro-stutter anomalies via precise stopwatch delta checks to build a visual profile of system consistency.

Bottleneck Tester

• Computes utilization thresholds across core components during resource testing.
• Compares memory queues, disk write speeds, and CPU cycles to determine balance efficiency.

5. SYSTEM RECOMMENDATIONS & ADAPTATIONS

Non-Blocking IO File Watching

The File Organizer Engine should use System.IO.FileSystemWatcher hooked directly to background event actions. Avoid using polling loops (Get-ChildItem updates every few seconds) to check folders; native file system events use fewer resources and ensure your data sorting triggers instantly when a file hits your downloads folder.

Log Caching for the Super Logger

The Super Logger utility should maintain an isolated storage array for processed entries. Reading raw Windows logs on every click can slow down the tool. Fetch the records once, process the hex code translations via an internal hash table look-up list, and hold the output in memory for smooth browsing inside the text window.

Latency Profiling (Jitterbug)

To capture real system latency spikes without creating high CPU overhead, use [System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch]::StartNew() inside an isolated micro-loop. By calculating the exact timing differences between clock checks, you can find hidden system interruptions caused by drivers or hardware without loading external monitoring programs.
